      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Public Works</strong><br>\nDomitian's commitment to improving Rome’s infrastructure was evident in his extensive building projects, including the completion of the Colosseum after the damage from a fire. He also built a new palace on the Palatine Hill and constructed the Forum Transitorium. These projects not only aimed to enhance Rome's grandeur but also provided employment for the populace, increasing his popularity among the working classes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nUnder Domitian, Rome engaged in several military campaigns to protect its borders and expand its territory. His forces battled against the Chatti, a Germanic tribe, and undertook a campaign in Britain. While these campaigns had mixed results, they helped Domitian cultivate an image of a warrior emperor defending and expanding the Roman Empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with the Senate</strong><br>\nDomitian’s relationship with the Roman Senate was strained. He saw the senatorial class as a threat to his authority and took measures to reduce its power. The Senate resented his autocratic ways and the lack of respect he showed for senatorial privileges. This tension would characterize his entire reign and eventually contribute to his downfall.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
