            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Religious and Cultural Policies</strong><br>\nDomitian was deeply religious and promoted the traditional Roman religion. He saw himself as the guardian of Roman religious practices and took the title of censor to control the morals and conduct of Rome’s citizens. He also insisted on being addressed as \"Dominus et Deus,\" meaning \"Lord and God,\" which reflected his autocratic and divine status in Roman society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Domitian's Downfall</strong><br>\nDespite initial popularity, Domitian’s autocratic rule, his behavior towards the Senate, and his acts of treason trials against perceived enemies made him many adversaries. Discontent grew among the elites, and in 96 AD, a court conspiracy involving his own officials led to his assassination. The Senate quickly declared him a public enemy and threw support behind his successor, Nerva.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Domitian</strong><br>\nDomitian's death marked the end of the Flavian Dynasty. Despite his autocratic rule, the empire prospered economically and militarily during his reign. However, his negative portrayal by contemporary historians, who were often linked to the senatorial class, skewed the perception of his legacy. Later, the empire would often reflect on Domitian's rule with disfavor, overshadowing his contributions to Rome's development.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
